kosha architectural-space

  -- the primary material of architecture: space itself.
  -- architecture does not build walls — it creates space bounded by walls.
  -- the wall is the means; the space is the end.
  --
  -- qualities of architectural space:
  --   enclosure:    how bounded the space feels. open field vs narrow alley vs cave.
  --   proportion:   ratio of dimensions. a room 3×3×3m feels different from 3×3×9m.
  --   scale:        relationship to human body. monumental vs intimate.
  --   light:        direction, quality, colour, intensity. natural vs artificial.
  --                 north light: cool, diffuse, consistent. south light: warm, directional.
  --   acoustic:     reverberation time. hard surfaces reflect, soft absorb.
  --   circulation:  how people move through and between spaces. the path.
  --
  -- space types:
  --   served space:   the room you inhabit. living, sleeping, working.
  --   servant space:  staircase, corridor, bathroom, mechanical room.
  --   threshold:      doorway, entrance, transition between spaces.
  --   void:           deliberate absence — courtyard, atrium, lightwell.
  --
  -- the section: architecture is understood in section (vertical cut) not just plan.
  --   plan shows horizontal arrangement. section shows spatial experience.
  --   a cathedral section: nave, triforium, clerestory — each a distinct space layer.
  --
  -- the promenade architecturale (Le Corbusier): movement through space as experience.
  -- the path is designed as a sequence of spatial events.
  -- entry → compression → release → compression → release → destination.

  "architecture-varga-vishesa"
  "engineering-varga-vishesa"
  "subanta-swarupa"

  "geometry-yukta"
  "human-yukta"
  "light-yukta acoustic-yukta"
